#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093352 :tutorial/content "Suppose we would like to find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t}, \\quad y=2\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/83/836757696669a0852812a63c8cafcf9ac3bddd73558f7b3e67c8427ec39c008a.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the arc length $L$ of a curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=x(t), \\quad y=y(t), \\qquad t\\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nis given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe intuition behind this formula is given at the end of this tutorial.\n\nAccording to the formula, the length of the blue arc is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fad3b2e7-80ac-52b4-bcc3-6ba5062e2d49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1078 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093353 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our first example, which was to find the arc length of the curve defined by\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t}, \\quad y=2\\sin{t}, \\quad t\\in\\left[ 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$\n\n![](images/83/836757696669a0852812a63c8cafcf9ac3bddd73558f7b3e67c8427ec39c008a.png)\n\nFirst, we divide the interval $\\left[ 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right]$ into small subintervals of equal length. Consider a part of the blue arc that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of that part be $\\Delta{s}$.\n\n![](images/35/3502e1055e3c22ef338dcfa015a4d603f808e9db5d3afea75391edddb440801a.png)\n\nWe can now denote the horizontal displacement as $\\Delta{x}$ and the vertical shift as $\\Delta{y}.$ \n\n![](images/ed/ed47d9cb8d8bce8514bc876870a5f3e175a87d2e59817ddbb3e10fd8094b24d3.png)\n\nTo approximate the length $\\Delta{s}$ we apply the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δs & ≈\\sqrt{{(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo approximate the total length $L,$ we need to sum up all of the little $\\Delta s$ terms, so\n\n$$\nL \\approx \\sum \\Delta s = \\sum \\sqrt{\\left( \\Delta{x} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\Delta{y} \\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nWe then take the limit as $\\Delta x, \\Delta y \\rightarrow 0.$ As we do this, the sum becomes a definite integral, and our approximation approaches the exact value of $L$. So, using the chain rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int \\sqrt{{(\\text{d}x)}^{2}+{(\\text{d}y)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\text{d}t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\text{d}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot {(\\text{d}t)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot {(\\text{d}t)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ea844379-a23b-53ff-abaf-1e0b3cf98214" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1082 :tutorial/title "The Intuition Behind the Main Formula"}]}
